#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""tmux-tab-bar: a status bar of up to 5 rows that groups tmux windows by project.

  tmux-tab-bar layout                       lay out the bar of every session that has a client
  tmux-tab-bar nav <direction> <client>     move that client's session to the tab left, right,
                                            up or down of its current one, as the bar draws them
  tmux-tab-bar move <left|right|up|down|newrow|auto> <client> [<window>] [--menu]
                                            carry a tab (default: the client's current one)
  tmux-tab-bar drop <src> <dst> <x> <line> <range> <client>      a tab dragged onto a tab
  tmux-tab-bar drop-row <src> <x> <line> <client>                a tab dragged onto a row's gutter
  tmux-tab-bar name-row <client> [<window>]  name the tab's custom row from @tab_row_name_in
  tmux-tab-bar menu <client> [<window>]      the Move menu; reset <client> clears the session

A repo with lanes (skills/lanes) gets its own row: a coloured header with the repo's name, its
lead checkout's tab first, then its lanes, then its other worktrees (agent worktrees). Every other
window goes on the shared row(s), last. Every row starts with the same gutter (status-left's
width, then the widest header), so every row's first tab starts in the same column. A tab is 32
cells wide, left-justified, with a dim `│` between two tabs; a row too full for that splits its
width evenly among its tabs. A kind spills into another row only when a slot would fall under 18
cells (9 on a phone), its tabs then spread evenly over its rows. Widths come from counts and the
client's width alone, so a title change never moves a tab or changes the row count (which would
resize every pane). Past 5 rows the smallest lane groups move onto the shared rows behind a
`┃repo▸` divider; still past 5, the last row ends in `+N`, which opens the window list. The
current window is always shown, under its own header: hidden, it takes a slot of its own group
or kind, else the `+N` row's last slot behind its own divider. A client narrower than 100 cells (the phone) gets short slots
and the bar at the bottom; a session's narrowest client decides its layout. Window numbers never
change: the bar only draws tabs in another order.

tmux/claude.conf runs `layout` from hooks (attach, detach, resize, a session switch, a window
opened, closed or selected), and tmux-claude-status runs it in its rate-limited pass, which
catches what no hook reports (a cwd change, a lane opened or closed, a swap-window or a
move-window -r, which fire no hook in tmux 3.7c) within about 4 s. Each window is classified
by its active pane's cwd, from the filesystem alone: no git call. Writes are per session: every
`status-format[0..N-1]` row (a session's first array write leaves the rows it didn't write blank),
then `status` (the row count, set only when it changes), the header names as `@tab_bar_g<n>`, and
`status-position`, `status-left` and `status-right` (set on the phone, unset on the desktop). A hash of all of it in
`@tab_bar_sig` makes an unchanged run write nothing. The window options `@tab_name` (`lead`, a
lane id, `repo:`, or `repo·name` for a laned tab moved out of its project's row), `@tab_group`
(the group's colour index) and `@tab_row_custom` (1 in a custom row, else unset, for the menus'
Name row…) are written only when they change.
Every write of a run goes to one `tmux source-file -` on stdin, one line per session with its sig
last, so a session that vanished mid-run (an error drops the rest of its line) records no sig.

Shift+arrows (claude.conf) run `nav`, which reads the client's session, its current window and
the session's `@tab_bar_nav` (each shown row's tabs with their columns, written by `layout` like
the rows) and selects one window. Left and right step in the order the bar draws the tabs,
wrapping; up and down go to the tab above or below (the one under the current tab's first
cell, else the nearest), wrapping. Rows count top to bottom on both
layouts (tmux draws row 0 on top even with the bar at the bottom). A window hidden in `+N`, or a
session with no `@tab_bar_nav`: left and right fall back to tmux's previous-window and
next-window, and up and down stay put.

Tabs can be arranged by hand. A window's `@tab_place` (a JSON object, parse_place) puts it in a
row (its project's, another project's, the shared row or a custom row) at a position. A row with
any tab placed in it is manual: its placed tabs by position, then the rest in auto order; other
rows stay auto. A placement in a project row that no window classifies into this pass falls back
to auto. A custom row (`c:<n>`) is a group like a project's, drawn right after the row it was made
below (else last among the groups), with a `▸` or `name▸` header in CUSTOM_COLOUR. A laned tab
outside its project's row is named `repo·name`. Every move (keys, drag, the Move menu) is one
primitive, put(): a tab goes before or after another in that tab's row, and every member of that
row gets a placement 0..n-1. The move then lays out at once under the layout lock (waiting for a
hook's run in flight, so the next queued move reads a fresh @tab_bar_nav) and copies each
changed placement into the restore records of the window's panes (open-sessions/<sid>.json,
field `place`), so tmux/claude-restore puts it back on the new window after tmux dies.

Nothing from a window title, a directory name or a lane record enters a format string: the rows
hold only window ids (`@N`), integers and fixed template text, names reach the bar through options
read plainly (`#{@tab_name}`), and titles with each `#` drawn as `♯`, so a `#[...]` in a title
draws as text and a `#` can't change a tab's width. No `%` either (tmux runs each row through
strftime). The script never writes to stdout or stderr (tmux puts a hook's output in the pane):
it fails open, leaving the bar as it was (tmux's default single row before the first layout),
with the traceback in the run dir. Linked into ~/.local/bin by setup-tmux.sh;
tests/setup/test_tmux_tab_bar.py holds the cases.
"""
import os
import sys

if __name__ == "__main__":      # say nothing, ever: tmux shows a hook's output in the pane
    _null = os.open(os.devnull, os.O_WRONLY)
    os.dup2(_null, 1)
    os.dup2(_null, 2)

